          ],
          "grounding_supports": [
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": null,
                "end_index": 99,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "When someone is feeling nauseated or ill, we often say they look \"pale\" or \"green around the gills\""
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                1,
                0
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 101,
                "end_index": 256,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "While you might not literally turn the color of a cartoon character, the \"sickly green\" or sallow, ghostly white look is a very real physiological response"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                1,
                2,
                3
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 260,
                "end_index": 391,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "The phenomenon is caused by a combination of blood vessel mechanics, optical illusions, and the way our brains perceive human faces"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4,
                5
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 406,
                "end_index": 594,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Why We Turn Pale (The Vascular Science)\nThe primary reason you lose your color when sick\u2014particularly with nausea, pain, fever, or shock\u2014comes down to your **autonomic nervous system**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                6,
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 598,
                "end_index": 752,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "When your body senses a threat (such as a stomach bug, a sudden drop in blood pressure, or extreme anxiety), it triggers a mild \"fight-or-flight\" response"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                7,
                8
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 755,
                "end_index": 908,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Vasoconstriction:** To protect you, your body narrows (constricts) the blood vessels in your skin and extremities (like your face, fingers, and toes)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                9,
                7,
                8
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 911,
                "end_index": 1100,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Redirecting Blood:** It diverts that warm, oxygen-rich red blood away from the surface of your skin and channels it toward your vital internal organs\u2014like your brain, heart, and lungs"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                10,
                5
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1103,
                "end_index": 1303,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **The Result:** Since oxygenated red blood in your superficial capillaries is what gives your skin its warm, healthy, pinkish undertones, draining that blood leaves your skin looking pale and clammy"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                1,
                9
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1543,
                "end_index": 1657,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Mixing Blue and Yellow:** Human skin naturally contains yellowish and olive pigments (melanin and carotenoids)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                9,
                10
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1659,
                "end_index": 1770,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "At the same time, the veins resting just beneath your skin scatter light in a way that makes them appear bluish"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                3
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1772,
                "end_index": 1881,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "When the bright red blood is drained from your capillaries, the red no longer \"masks\" these underlying colors"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                9
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1883,
                "end_index": 2004,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "The yellow of your skin pigment combines with the blue-reflecting veins, creating a dull, greenish-yellow, or sallow cast"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                1,
                10
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2006,
                "end_index": 2086,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Visual Contrast:** Our eyes and brains are highly sensitive to facial colors"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                3
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2088,
                "end_index": 2296,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Because red and green are complementary colors on the color wheel, when our brain registers a sudden, dramatic *subtraction* of red tones from a face, it naturally overcompensates by perceiving a greenish hue"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                2
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2300,
                "end_index": 2513,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "In fact, facial colorimetry studies\u2014including space sickness research\u2014have found that shifts in the \"green channel\" of facial skin are among the most reliable objective indicators of nausea and motion sickness"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                11
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2746,
                "end_index": 2961,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Severe Anemia (Historically called \"Green Sickness\"):** In the 18th and 19th centuries, doctors frequently diagnosed a condition called *chlorosis* (or \"green sickness\"), primarily in young, undernourished women"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                11
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2963,
                "end_index": 3022,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Today, we understand this was severe iron-deficiency anemia"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                11,
                12
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3024,
                "end_index": 3180,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Because the body lacked hemoglobin (the iron-rich protein that makes red blood cells red), patients' skin developed a distinct, sallow, greenish-yellow tint"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                13
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3182,
                "end_index": 3329,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Jaundice and Liver Issues:** If your liver, gallbladder, or bile ducts are struggling, a compound called bilirubin can build up in your tissues"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                14
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3331,
                "end_index": 3485,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "While classic jaundice looks yellow, a severe or prolonged buildup can oxidise into biliverdin, giving the skin and eyes a distinctly greenish-yellow tint"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                15,
                13
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3487,
                "end_index": 3717,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Cyanosis (Lack of Oxygen):** If your lungs or heart are struggling and your blood lacks sufficient oxygen, your skin can take on a bluish-gray-green cast (cyanosis), which is most visible on the lips, gums, and under the nails"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                1,
                10
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3725,
                "end_index": 3876,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "### An Evolutionary Warning System\nInterestingly, the human brain has evolved to be exceptionally good at detecting these subtle shifts in facial color"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                10
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3878,
                "end_index": 4085,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "From an evolutionary standpoint, being able to quickly spot when someone else looks \"pale and green\" allowed our ancestors to identify sick individuals and avoid contagious pathogens before they could spread"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            }
          ],
